--TEST--
a `#[Test]` method that is `static`, that is not `public`, that returns anything but `void` or that has no body is refused where it is declared, and two sharing one name with it — `rule:testing/test-attribute`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// covers: lang:testing/a-test-is-a-method-marked-test
// `rule:testing/test-attribute`: these are compile errors, not skipped tests. Four of the
// six are about the declaration rather than the payload — a `#[Test]`
// that is `static`, that is not `public`, that returns anything but
// `void`, or that has no body — and they share one code because they are
// one question: the runner constructs the class and calls the member with
// no arguments and no result. The fifth, two `#[Test]` methods sharing one
// name, is the `E0304` an option written twice already draws.
use Core\Test;

// The accepted half first, so a position that stops being accepted fails
// here rather than as a missing refusal. The two plain helpers beside it
// are the other half of that: a `static` and a `private` method with no
// `#[Test]` on it is nothing this pass has an opinion about.
class Accepted {
    #[Test]
    public function aTestIsAPublicVoidInstanceMethod(): void {}

    public static function aPlainStaticHelper(): int { return 1; }

    private function aPlainPrivateHelper(): string { return "x"; }
}

class Refused {
    #[Test]
    public static function aTestIsNotStatic(): void {}

    #[Test]
    private function aTestIsPublic(): void {}

    #[Test]
    protected function protectedIsNotPublicEither(): void {}

    #[Test]
    public function aTestReturnsVoid(): int { return 1; }

    // One declaration, three refusals: the family is one question asked of
    // three parts of a signature, so each part answers for itself.
    #[Test]
    private static function everyShapeAtOnce(): string { return "x"; }

    // A test is reported by its own name, so two cannot share one.
    #[Test]
    public function twiceOver(): void {}

    #[Test]
    public function twiceOver(): void {}
}

// An `abstract` test has no body, so the runner has nothing to call.
abstract class RefusedBodiless {
    #[Test]
    abstract public function aTestHasABody(): void;
}

echo "unreachable\n";
--EXPECT--

